            <div xml:id="d295">

                <pb facs="0622" n="590" sameAs="frus:frus1964-68v18/pg590"/>

                <head>295. Intelligence Memorandum Prepared in the Central Intelligence
                        Agency<anchor n="1" xml:id="d295-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 0830/66</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1966-05-28">May 28, 1966</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#center">EGYPTIAN-SOVIET RELATIONS</p>
                <p rendition="#center">Summary</p>
                <p>Egypt and the USSR may be moving toward a period
                    of closer cooperation in the pursuit of their common objectives in the Arab
                    world. Nasir’s reviving antagonism toward conservative and moderate Arab
                    leaders, his concurrent tendency to try to unify “progressive and revolutionary”
                    Arab forces, and his renewed hostility toward US and British policy in the
                    Middle East have almost certainly increased his readiness to collaborate with
                    Moscow. Moscow’s present leadership will be cautiously receptive to
                    opportunities Nasir may offer for extending Soviet influence in the area. They
                    already appear to be preparing <pb facs="0623" n="591" xml:id="pg591"/> to work
                    somewhat more closely with him than in the past in espousing his kind of Arab
                    nationalism, socialism, and opposition to Western influence. There are, however,
                    definite limitations on such cooperation well-recognized by both sides.</p>
                <p>[Here follow 17 paragraphs of more detailed discussion.]</p>
            </div>
        </body>
